n{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b00be353-af3c-5697-8948-437078c60988"}] :activity/title "Computing the Singular Values of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434790}]}
